Instruction: Read the following segment of a merger agreement and determine which option best characterizes whether the concept "ability to consummate" is subject to Material Adverse Effect (MAE) carveouts.

Question: Is the "ability to consummate" concept subject to the carveouts provided in the definition of Material Adverse Effect?
Option A: No
Option B: Yes

Merger Agreement Excerpt (Pages 12-13):
"Company Material Adverse Effect" means any event, change, occurrence or circumstance that, either individually or in the aggregate, has had or would reasonably be expected to have a material adverse effect on the business, financial condition, operational results, or the ability of the Company to consummate the transactions contemplated hereby. In assessing whether a Company Material Adverse Effect has occurred, the following exceptions shall apply solely to general business performance factors: 
   (i) Fluctuations in overall economic conditions, including changes in interest rates, exchange rates, and market liquidity;
   (ii) Changes in applicable GAAP or statutory accounting principles;
   (iii) Force majeure events, such as natural disasters, pandemics, and acts of war;
   (iv) Broad industry-wide downturns.
Notwithstanding the foregoing carveouts, any effect that directly prevents, materially delays, or impairs the Company’s ability to consummate the transactions shall not be subject to these exceptions. Furthermore, no provision of this Agreement shall be construed to mitigate the impact of such an effect on the consummation process.

Choices:

