Instruction: Read the following segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.

Question: Is the “ability to consummate” concept subject to Material Adverse Effect carveouts?
Option A: No
Option B: Yes

Merger Agreement Excerpt:
"Definition: 'Company Adjusted Material Impact' means any event, development, or occurrence that, individually or in aggregate, (a) would materially impair, delay, or prevent the Company from consummating the transactions contemplated by this Agreement, or (b) would have a material adverse effect on the Company’s overall business operations. Notwithstanding the foregoing, for the purposes of determining whether an event qualifies as a Company Adjusted Material Impact under clause (b), the following factors shall be excluded: 
   (i) fluctuations in general market interest and exchange rates;
   (ii) industry-wide downturns or general economic slowdowns affecting all participants without any disproportionate impact;
   (iii) normal seasonal variations and standard commodity price volatility;
   (iv) changes in applicable accounting standards or laws unless such changes are uniquely attributed to the transactions contemplated herein.
However, it is expressly provided that none of the aforementioned exclusions shall apply with respect to any event, development, or occurrence that directly impairs, delays, or prevents the Company’s ability to consummate the transactions contemplated by this Agreement."

Based on the above, indicate whether the carveout exclusions apply to the 'ability to consummate' component of the definition.
Choices:
